|~ `::regex` and what is declared under it: the refusal of a
   payload of another kind, and `toRegex`, which answers the regex itself. The verbs
   that read text by a regex live under `::string`.
   The rules it holds: [QSTXT#regex]. ~|

:regex
  |~~ A regex, `#"\d+ms"`, which the verbs of a string read text by:
      whether a match stands, `"took 12ms" | contains #"\d+ms"`, the
      first match and its groups, `match`, the groups it names, `capture`,
      the text of every match, `scan`, the text put instead of every
      match, `replace`, and the pieces between the matches, `split`.

      What stands between the quotes reaches the engine as it is written:
      `\d` is a digit and `\.` a dot, with no backslash doubled, and `\"`
      is a quote that does not end the literal. The flags follow the
      closing quote, each once, `i` to ignore case, `m` for `^` and `$` at
      every line and `s` for a dot that matches a line break,
      `#"^error"im`. A regex reads text by code points, as Unicode names
      characters, so `#"\p{L}+"` is a run of letters of any script.
      The dialect is the one of JavaScript in its edition of 2018, which
      the engine reads alike on every platform: a group of flags,
      `(?i:…)`, and a group name written twice, which only later engines
      read, do not parse, nor does a regex the engine cannot read, and
      the refusal says why, naming the repair of a habit of another
      dialect, flags written inside or a backslash before a character
      that needs none, `\-`, `\:`.

      A regex is no string: `split "."` cuts a text at its dots, and
      `split #"[.,;]"` at any of three marks. Text becomes a regex by
      `toRegex`, the flags its input `flags`, `"error" | toRegex "i"`,
      and a regex prints as its literal, which `print` writes. JSON
      writes that literal as a string, which `parse | open` reads back,
      and regexes rank after strings, by their text and then their flags.
      ::snippet~(::string | manifest)
      ::link~(::string/match | doc) ::link~(::string/capture | doc)
      ::link~(::string | doc) ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|
  ::kind
    | accepts |~~ A regex, read as itself. ~~| ::regex
    | throws :PayloadKindNotTakenError
        |~~ `::regex` reads its payload as the regex it is; text becomes
            one by `toRegex`, `"\\d+" | toRegex`.
        ~~|
    | proves :isARegex
        |~~ `#"\d+"` is a regex. ~~|
        ~(#"\d+" | tag | eq ::regex)
    | proves :readsAsWritten
        |~~ A backslash reaches the engine as written, and `\"` writes a
            quote.
        ~~|
        ~(["12" "a.b" "say \"hi\""] | map ~(contains #"\d|\.|\"hi\"") | eq [true true true])
    | proves :takesItsFlagsAfterItsQuote
        |~~ The flags follow the closing quote and change how the engine
            reads.
        ~~|
        ~(["ERROR here" "a\nerror"] | get [~(get /0 | contains #"error"i) ~(get /1 | contains #"^error"m)] | eq [true true])
    | proves :readsLettersOfAnyScript
        |~~ A regex reads text by code points, a class of Unicode among
            it.
        ~~|
        ~("данные 42" | match #"\p{L}+" | eq ["данные"])
    | proves :holdsOneDialect
        |~~ A group of flags and a group name written twice do not parse,
            on every platform.
        ~~|
        ~(["#\"(?i:a)b\"" "#\"(?<y>a)|(?<y>b)\""] | map ~(parse !| tag) | eq [::quote/ParseError ::quote/ParseError])
    | proves :namesTheRepairOfAHabit
        |~~ The refusal of a needless backslash names where a backslash
            stands.
        ~~|
        ~("#\"a\\:b\"" | parse !| message | contains "a backslash stands before")
    | proves :readsBackFromJson
        |~~ JSON writes a regex as its literal, which `parse | open` reads
            back.
        ~~|
        ~(#"\d+"i | :p it | json | parseJson | parse | open | eq p)
    | proves :printsAsItsLiteral
        |~~ A regex prints as its literal and reads back as itself. ~~|
        ~(#"\"(\d+)\""i | :p it | print | parse | open | eq p)
    | proves :isNoString
        |~~ A regex is no string, so a text cuts at a dot and a regex at
            any mark.
        ~~|
        ~("a.b,c" | get [~(split ".") ~(split #"[.,]")] | eq [["a" "b,c"] ["a" "b" "c"]])
    | proves :ranksAfterStrings
        |~~ Regexes rank after strings and before keywords, by their
            text.
        ~~|
        ~([:k #"b" "z" #"a"] | sort | eq ["z" #"a" #"b" :k])
    | proves :PayloadKindNotTakenError/refusesAString
        |~~ A string payload is refused; `toRegex` reads text as a regex.
        ~~|
        ~(::regex"a" !| get [~(tag) ~(actualTag)] | eq [::regex/PayloadKindNotTakenError ::string])

:regex/toRegex
  |~~ Answers the regex itself, so `map ~toRegex` turns regexes and the
      strings that hold their sources into regexes; given flags, as
      `string/toRegex` takes them, it answers its source with those flags,
      `#"a" | toRegex "i"` answering `#"a"i`.
      ::link~(::string/toRegex | doc)
  ~~|
  ::fn
    | takes :flags
        |~~ The flags of the regex written in a string, the regex's own
            where none is given.
        ~~|
        ~(query /0 null) #[::null ::string]
    | throws :flags/KindNotTakenError
        |~~ The flags are written in a string, `toRegex "i"`. ~~|
    | proves :flags/KindNotTakenError/refusesANumber
        |~~ Flags that are no string are refused. ~~|
        ~(#"a" | toRegex 5 !| tag | eq ::regex/toRegex/flags/KindNotTakenError)
    | throws :flags/NotFlagsError
        |~~ The flags are `i`, `m` and `s`, each once, `toRegex "im"`. ~~|
        ~(wires :actualTag |~~ The kind of the value refused. ~~| ::tag
          | wires :actualValue |~~ The value refused. ~~|)
    | proves :flags/NotFlagsError/refusesALetterOfNoFlag
        |~~ A letter that names no flag is refused. ~~|
        ~(#"a" | toRegex "z" !| tag | eq ::regex/toRegex/flags/NotFlagsError)
    | returns |~~ The regex itself, or its source with the flags. ~~| ::regex
    | proves :takesFlagsAsAStringDoes
        |~~ A regex given flags answers its source with them. ~~|
        ~(#"a" | toRegex "i" | eq ("a" | toRegex "i"))
    | proves :answersItself
        |~~ A regex answers itself, so regexes and their sources turn into
            regexes.
        ~~|
        ~([#"\d" "\\d"] | map ~toRegex | eq [#"\d" #"\d"])
    | impl ::builtin{}

